editorialMd:
Build foundValues

`root` is a binary search tree with no repeated values. In this tree, all values in a left branch are smaller than its parent, and all values in a right branch are larger. Find values from `k` through `k` + 12, including both ends. Put them in `foundValues` from smallest to largest. Skip branches whose values cannot be in this range. An empty tree gives an empty `foundValues`.

Return the answer

Count the numbers in `foundValues` that are at least `k` (greater than or equal to `k`). Store the count in `atLeastK`. Complete `catalogBranchesThresholdFile` so it returns `atLeastK`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `foundValues` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(h + r); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the number of nodes; h is the longest root-to-leaf path in the tree; a leaf is a node with no children; r is the number of tree values found. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function catalogBranchesThresholdFile(root, k) {
  const a=root; let out=[];
  function visit(node){if(!node)return;if(node.val>k)visit(node.left);if(node.val>=k&&node.val<=k+12)out.push(node.val);if(node.val<k+12)visit(node.right);}visit(a);
  return out.filter(x=>x>=k).length;
}


